    <h1>Exercise Name: Close Neutral-Grip Pull-Up (Parallel Grip Pull-Up)</h1>
   <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>

This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Close Neutral-Grip Pull-Ups (V-Bar / Parallel Grip Pull-Ups) 
    viewed from behind. The video highlights the upper and middle back muscles—specifically the traps, 
    rhomboids, and lats (Trapezius, Rhomboids, Latissimus Dorsi)—in glowing orange and blue, illustrating
     how a close, palms-facing-inward grip engages the back muscles through full range pulling motion.

<pre>
 <b>Exercise Name:</b> Close Neutral-Grip Pull-Up (Parallel Grip Pull-Up)
 <b>Target Muscles:</b> Upper and Middle Back (Trapezius, Rhomboids, Latissimus Dorsi)
 <b>Secondary Muscles:</b> Biceps (Biceps Brachii), Brachialis, Forearms, Rear Deltoids, Core
 </pre>

</div></div>

<h1>

How to Perform (Step-by-Step)
</h1> 1. SetupGrab a neutral-grip handle setup (palms facing each other) on a pull-up bar or frame, keeping your hands close together (roughly shoulder-width or closer). Hang with fully extended arms and a tight core.
 
 <u><b><h4>2. Pulling Phase</h4></b></u>Exhale as you pull your body upward by driving your elbows down and back toward your sides. Lead with your chest and pull until your upper chest or chin reaches handle level.
 
 <u><b><h4>3. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>Pause for a split second at the top position, squeezing your back and shoulder blades together.
 
 <u><b><h4>4. Lowering Phase</h4></b></u>Inhale as you slowly lower your body back down under control over 2 to 3 seconds until your arms reach full extension at the bottom.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 
 <b>◉Joint-Friendly Grip:</b> Neutral wrist placement puts far less stress on the shoulders, elbows, and wrists compared to overhand pull-ups or underhand chin-ups.<br>
 
 <b>◉Balanced Back & Bicep Loading:</b> Combines heavy lat and upper back engagement with strong bicep leverage, allowing you to lift body weight with maximum power.<br>
 
 <b>◉Core & Grip Strength:</b> Carrying full body weight builds significant grip endurance and requires active core stabilization throughout the movement.



<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 8 – 12 | 90 seconds |
| Endurance | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 (or bodyweight max) | 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 4 – 6 (weighted if needed) | 2 – 3 minutes |

Keep your torso still and avoid swinging your legs or using momentum to kick yourself up to the handles.

